## 1. Introduction
Recently, splitting water by solar-driven photocatalytic techniques and hydrogen generation promises a green/clean alternative for conventional fossil fuel technologies. To date, numerous semiconductors like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , ZnO, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ have emerged to produce $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ from water splitting under sunlight illumination $[1-$ 4]. However, due to drawbacks from the wide band gap, low photon absorption, low specific surface area, poor electron-hole separation and high recombination, many efforts have been made to develop a highly efficient solar-driven photocatalyst by reducing these bottlenecks.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are microporous crystalline semiconductor materials comprised of metal oxo clusters as inorganic nodes and organic linkers [5–7]. Because of high specific surface area, regular pore size and versatile structures, MOFs have been extensively utilized in many fields such as gas storage, drug delivery, sensing and catalysts [8–10]. In addition, recently MOFs have attracted the attention of the research community, especially in photocatalytic water splitting, organic dye decolorization, and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction [1,2,11]. The pioneering study for the photocatalytic application of MOF materials in water splitting was reported by Kataoka (2009) [6]. However, shortcomings of low light harvesting, limited active sites and mass transfer resistance reduced the photocatalytic performance. In this regard, various MOFs were examined and designed to further enhance the photocatalytic activities such as heterojunction with other MOFs, metal nanoparticles, polymers, graphene, et cetera [12]. More recently, reports related with hollow MOFs have increased attention owing to improved properties with respect to conventional MOFs, including more active sites, improved light absorption due to scattering of light in cavity, facilitated transfer of charge carriers, and decrease in mass transfer resistance owing to decrease in diffusion length in the MOF shell [5,13,14]. The most widely used method for the fabrication of hollow MOFs (i.e., core–shell) is the utilization of solid template and afterwards removing of this template from MOF structure by employing an additional post-treatment process [8]. Among different templates such as $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , polymers, and metals, polymers are favorable for the synthesis of hollow MOFs owing to mild fabrication conditions and easy removal from the core. Oh and coworkers prepared the first hollow ZIF-8 MOF using polymer template (polystyrene) [15]. To date, numerous hollow MOFs were prepared successfully by using polymers like polystyrene (PS), polyacrylonitrile (PAN), or nanofibers as a starting template [16].
Among the above mentioned fabrication techniques, carbonbased hollow MOF photocatalysts have been attracting interest owing to their improvement in stability and electrical conductivity, thus increasing electron mobility and inhibiting recombination of charge carriers [17]. Generally, MOFs are employed for synthesis of carbon-based catalysts because they include carbon, nitrogen, and metals abundantly in their structures in which the hollow MOFs are formed by carbonization to enhance catalytic performance. However, there are limited MOF precursors for the formation of hollow structures using such recipes [18]. Therefore, there is still a great need for development of new carbon sources for improvement of carbon-based MOF photocatalysts. Polydopamine (pDA), having amino and cathechol groups, could easily adhere on different surfaces, especially on metal surfaces, due to chelate formation ability between pDA and metal ions [18–20]. pDA is not only utilized as a starting template to fabricate well-defined MOF structures, but also used as an $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon source thanks to the existence of amine groups in its structure. Therefore, construction of pDA templates having carbon and nitrogen is an effective way to benefit from light inside the cavity and obtain highly efficient catalyst performance. Because, direct interaction between the light confined in the cavity and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon in the inner side of the hollow structure having high conductivity would result in efficient electron-hole separation and inhibition of electron-hole recombination.
To the best of our knowledge, hollow $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon incorporated UiO-66 metal-organic framework (H-UiO-66) nanostructures using multifunctional pDA (i.e., containing catechol and amine groups) as a starting template has never been reported for PEC water splitting until now. In this study, novel hollow Zr-MOF (HUiO-66) spherical shape-defined photocatalysts were developed by one-pot integration of $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphitic carbon followed by carbonization of a pDA core. Important improvements in the photocatalytic performance of hollow UiO-66 nanostructures were attained under visible LED illumination due to the unique welldefined/monodispersed morphology of photocatalysts, which combines advantages of having high catalytic active sites for the MOFs shell, scattering of light, and electrical conductivity of the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon layer. Moreover, hollow structure enhances light harvesting due to trapping efficiency of light inside the cavity; also, incorporation of $N\cdot$ -doped carbon into the inner side of hollow MOFs not only supplies well-ordered mesoporous structure but also provides better electron transfer with prolonged life time of charge carriers for remarkable enhancement of PEC water splitting.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals
Dopamine hydrochloride, ethanol $(\geq98\ \%$ , ammonium hydroxide solution $(\sim25\ \%\ \mathrm{NH}_{3}$ basis), zirconium(IV) chloride $\left(\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4},\ge\right)$ $99.5\mathrm{~\%~}$ , terephthalic acid (TPA, $98\ \%$ , $\mathbb{N},N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethyl formamide (DMF, $99.8~\%$ , sodium sulfate $(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{4}.$ , anhydrous, $\geq99.0\mathrm{~\%}$ , and Nafion solution were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Germany). Deionized water was obtained from Millipore Direct-Q 3 water purification system (Germany)
## 2.2. Synthesis of N-doped carbon incorporated hollow UiO-66 MOFs
N-doped carbon incorporated hollow MOFs (H-UiO-66) were synthesized by a two-step method. The first step consisted of fabrication of polydopamine (pDA) nanoparticles. Briefly, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of dopamine hydrochloride was dissolved in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water by ultrasonication for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Dopamine solution was then added into $4~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol including $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ammonium hydroxide solution 0 $2.5\%$ by weight). The resulting solution was stirred for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature and pDA nanoparticles were formed. The product was washed several times with ethanol and water to remove impurities and unreacted components, followed by vacuum drying at $60~^{\circ}C$ pDA nanoparticles were used as a template for UiO-66 MOF structure in the second step of the synthesis. 1 mg of pDA nanoparticles were dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF by ultrasonication. Then, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{zr}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ and $2{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of TPA were added into DMF solution and the resulting mixture was stirred continuously for $130~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $140^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ The obtained product, pDA/UiO-66 MOF core/shell structure, was washed several times with ethanol and water, and subsequently dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}C$ pDA/UiO-66 MOF core/shell nanostructures were then calcinated at $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with $1\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ heating rate for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon incorporated hollow UiO-66 MOFs were fabricated. Bare UiO-66 MOF nanostructures were also synthesized without pDA nanoparticles for comparison.
## 2.3. Characterization
Morphological characterization of the samples was performed with a field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, Hitachi SU5000, Japan) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM-2100F, Japan). Crystal structures of the samples were examined with X-ray Diffractometry (XRD, Rigaku MiniFlex 600, Japan). Optical and band properties of the samples were determined using an ultraviolet–visible spectrophotometer (UV–Vis, Shimadzu-1601, Japan). Surface composition characterization of the samples was performed by an X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS, Thermo-K-Alpha-Monochromated Spectrometer, USA). Electrochemical characterization of the samples was carried out using a potentiostat/galvanostat (Gamry Reference3000, USA). Electrolyte of 0.1 M ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ was used and a three-electrode configuration was exercised for all electrochemical measurements. The FTO glass modified with the samples, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{A g}}/{\mathsf{A g C l}}$ , and Pt wire were employed as working, reference, and counter electrodes, respectively. Modification of the FTO glass was described in our previous study [19]. Briefly, $1~\mathrm{mg}$ of the sample was dispersed in an equivolume mixture of ethanol and water including $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution $5\%$ by weight) by ultrasonication. Of this dispersion, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ was drop-coated on $0.3~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ area of the FTO glass and dried on a hot plate. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements were performed under dark and illuminated conditions to reveal the photoresponses of the samples. Tafel slopes, applied bias photonto-current efficiency (ABPE), and incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) curves were built from the data obtained from LSV. Current density versus time measurement was carried out to test the photostability of $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon incorporated hollow UiO-66 MOF. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurements were conducted to determine charge transfer resistances and further investigate the band properties of the samples. Nyquist plots were constructed through the $0.01{-}100000\ \mathrm{Hz}$ frequency range with an AC voltage of $10~\mathrm{mV}$ under dark and illuminated conditions. Mott–Schottky measurements were performed under $10~\mathrm{Hz}$ frequency. The reusability/stability tests of H-UiO-66 photoanodes were done by four successive PEC $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolutions under LED irradiation. The photoanodes were washed with $2{\mathrm{mL}}$ of water three times and dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ between successive PEC $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution experiments.
## 3. Results and discussion
Recently, carbon-based hollow MOF heterogeneous photocatalysts with well-defined morphologies have attracted the attention of scientists due to the improvement in stability and electrical conductivity, thus increasing electron mobility and inhibiting recombination of photogenerated charge carriers. For this purpose, novel H-UiO-66 nanosphere photocatalysts were developed by one-pot integration of $N\cdot$ -doped graphitic carbon followed by carbonization of the pDA core (Fig. 1A). Note that the bio-inspired pDA cores carrying carbon and nitrogen sources were utilized as multifunctional starting templates for the synthesis of H-UiO-66 nanostructures with spherical shape-defined morphologies. Moreover, the hollow morphology together with the light confined in the cavity and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon provided an efficient electronhole separation for highly enhanced PEC water splitting.
Fig. 1B shows the SEM photographs of pDA NPs, indicating the narrow size distribution $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ in size) and uniform spherical morphologies. After one-pot deposition of UiO-66 MOFs onto multifunctional pDA NPs and annealing at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the UiO-66 heterostructures with hollow spherical morphologies was obtained with quite narrow size distributions $(120~\pm~5~\mathrm{{\nm}})$ (Fig. 1C). As a result of thermal treatment at high temperature, well-defined H-UiO-66 heterostructures with the shell thickness of $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ were achieved, which is a unique value to reduce the transfer distance of photogenerated charge carriers (Fig. 1C, inset) [21]. The HRTEM images of H-UiO-66 heterostructures clearly revealed the uniform shell thickness of the UiO-66 and completely wrapping of the starting pDA templates with no aggregates (Fig. 1D). Additionally, the chemical composition and the presence of O, C, and Zr elements on hollow UiO-66 heterostructures were characterized by the EDX mapping technique (Fig. 1E). The obtained results showed that the corresponding main elements (O, C, and Zr) were uniformly distributed over the H-UiO-66 heterostructures, which clearly demonstrated the successful fabrication of novel NPs with preserved well-defined spherical morphologies (Fig. 1E and Fig. S1).
The chemical composition of H-UiO-66 MOFs was analyzed by XPS, and the presence of Zr, O, N, and C elements with good intensities were marked on the XPS survey spectra (Fig. 2A). As seen in Fig. 2B, two peaks $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ ) related to the spin orbitals of H-UiO-66 MOFs were recorded.
Fig. 2C shows the deconvolution of O 1s of hollow MOF nanostructures, representing the successful formation of $Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{~-~}0\mathrm{~-~}Z\mathrm{r}$ $(529.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ , $\scriptstyle0-{\mathsf{C}}=0$ $(532.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and $Z\Gamma-0{-}C$ bonds $(530.8~\mathrm{eV})$ in consequence of the bridged O onto secondary building units (SBUs), carboxylate groups of organic $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ linker, and immobilized metal-affinity sites, respectively [22]. Beside this, the deconvolution of C 1s spectra of hollow MOF nanostructures represented three main peaks corresponding to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ (284.7 eV), ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-N}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0$ $(285.8~\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\scriptstyle0-{\mathsf{C}}=0$ $(288.3~\mathrm{eV})$ bonds (Fig. 2D). Furthermore, the XRD analysis of H-UiO-66 nanostructures demonstrated the characteristic peaks indexed to the crystalline nature of UiO-66 MOFs as well (Fig. 2E) [23]. Moreover, the thermogravimetric (TG) analysis clearly confirmed the assumption regarding the formation of a $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphitic carbon layer in the structure of HUiO-66 nanostructures (Fig. 2F) in which only $2.6\%$ of weight loss was recorded in the case of H-UiO-66, while the losses were $52\%$ and $53\%$ for bare pDA and pDA@UiO-66 (before calcination), respectively.
The UV spectra of pristine UiO-66 and H-UiO-66 nanostructures show the intrinsic absorption bands at 240 and $280~\mathrm{{nm}}$ [24,25], with the difference of the intensities of these absorption bands with respect to each other (Fig. 3A-B). For the determination of the band gap values of materials, Tauc plots were constructed using the following equation [26]:
$$
\left(\alpha h\nu\right)^{1/\gamma}=B\left(h\nu-E_{g}\right)
$$
where, $\alpha,h,\nu,\gamma,B$ , and $E_{g}$ represent the absorption coefficient, Planck’s constant, frequency, and transition factor (1/2 for direct and 2 for indirect transitions), a constant, and band gap value, respectively. For both of the pristine UiO-66 and H-UiO-66 nanostructures, indirect transition was taken into consideration [27], and the band gap values were found to be 2.8 and $2.5\mathrm{eV}$ for pristine UiO-66 and H-UiO-66, respectively (Fig. 3C). This decrease indicates that the light absorption ability of H-UiO-66 is increased by altering the spherical shape-defined morphology as well as cavity of the hollow structure in which the light confined in the cavity and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon provided an efficient electron-hole separation.
The LSV measurements revealed that photoresponse of H-UiO66 under LED light reached a maximum current density of $10.95~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ at $1.53~\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, whereas pristine UiO-66 under LED light and H ZrMOF under dark showed insignificant responses of 0.0131 and $0.0281~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, at the same potential (Fig. 4A). Tafel slopes of the photoanodes were calculated from LSV measurements and presented in Fig. 4B. The slopes of HUiO-66 and pristine UiO-66 under illumination and H-UiO-66 under dark were calculated as 34.92, 95.4, and 96.1 mV/dec, respectively. The slope of H-UiO-66 under LED light was found to be substantially lower than that of pristine UiO-66 under illumination and H-UiO-66 under dark conditions, suggesting that charge transfer and photoresponsivity of the synthesized photoanode is superior with respect to its counterparts, which is also in accordance with LSV measurements. Applied bias-to-current efficiency (ABPE) plots of the photoanodes were shown in Fig. 4C. Pristine UiO-66 under LED light and H-UiO-66 under dark conditions showed no considerable efficiency values. The highest ABPE value of $4.9\%$ was achieved with H-UiO-66 under LED light at $0.84\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. These results prove that photoconversion efficiency of H-UiO66 was significantly improved. For further investigation of light conversion efficiency of the photoanodes, incident photon-tocurrent efficiency (IPCE) values were calculated and presented in Fig. 4D. H-UiO-66 under LED light exhibited a giant IPCE value of $377\%$ in the visible region ranging from 450 to $525\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength. This phenomenon is another indicator of the extraordinary light conversion ability of H-UiO-66 photoanodes, in which the IPCE greater than $100\%$ could be attributed to the generation of more $\mathrm{e^{-}-h^{+}}$ pairs per incident photon as well [28]. The inset in Fig. 4D shows the IPCE curves of pristine UiO-66 under LED light and HUiO-66 under dark conditions, achieving $0.99\%$ and $0.45\%$ conversion efficiency. These results show that the photoresponse of H
UiO-66 photoanode is considerably improved in the visible region, and generation of charge carriers is greatly accelerated. Finally, the improved visible light-sensitive PEC water splitting performance of H-UiO-66 photoanodes could be attributed to the main advantages of the one-pot synthesis method of hollow MOFs using multifunctional pDA as follows: i) the hollow morphology provided superior incident photon scattering and multi-reflection of photon inside the MOF cavity; ii) presence of N-doped carbon incorporated morphology facilitated the absorption of water molecules as well as the $\pi$ -polar interaction between water and carbon; and iii) the reduced bang-gap led to the optical localization of light within H-UiO-66 clusters. Furthermore, the comparison of the photoanode type, light source, and photocurrent density of various UiO-66 based composites were provided in which the highest current density was recorded for H-UiO-66 photoanodes $\cdot10.95~\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}2$ at
1.53 V vs RHE) (Table S1). Moreover, the reusability tests were performed for four cycles to assess the long-term stability of H-UiO-66 photoanodes (Fig. S2). As seen here, H-UiO-66 photoanodes showed an excellent reusability and stability at $1.53~\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) against cycle numbers under visible LED irradiation.
Electrochemical impedance spectrometry measurements were performed to investigate the charge transfer resistance and reveal band properties of the photoanodes. Nyquist plots of H-UiO-66 and pristine UiO-66 under LED and H-UiO-66 under dark conditions exhibited charge transfer resistance values of 26.62, 91.85, and $35.92~\mathrm{k}\Omega$ , respectively (Fig. 5A). The decreased charge transfer resistance value of H-UiO-66 under light is a clear proof of the excellent ability of generation and separation of charge transfer carriers. The Mott–Schottky plot of H-UiO-66 shown in Fig. 5B reveals the flat band potentials as $-0.35\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $10~\mathrm{Hz}$ frequency. Positive slope of the photoanode indicates that they are $n$ -type semiconductors [29], and it is known that the conduction band potentials $(E_{C B})$ are $0.1\mathrm{eV}$ more negative than their flatband potentials [30]. Thus the $E_{C B}$ value of H-UiO-66 is found to be $-0.45\ \mathrm{eV}$ The band gap of the photoanode found from Tauc plots was used to calculate their valence band potential $(E_{V B})$ , which was found to be $2.05{\mathrm{~eV}}.$ Furthermore, the life time of electrons in the H-UiO-66 photoanode was determined by Bode plot using the following Equation (2):
$$
t_{n}=1/(2P f_{\operatorname*{max}})
$$
where, $f_{m a x}$ is the frequency at which the low frequency peak appears, and $\tau_{\mathfrak{n}}$ is the electron life time [31]. As seen in Fig. 5C, the peak frequency shifted to a lower value, representing higher life time according to Equation (2). Hence, the electron life time was greater for H-UiO-66 $(46~\mathrm{ms})$ than for pristine UiO-66 $\cdot18\mathrm{~ms})$ , clearly demonstrating inhibition in electron-hole recombination, leading to enrichment in charge transfer through the rapid electron transfer ability of $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphitic carbon layer and prolonged life time of light inside the hollow structure. Moreover, considerable depression in PL intensity of H-UiO-66 compared to pristine UiO66 could be attributed to the N-doped graphitic carbon in the inner side as well as the hollow morphology, which led to an improved light scattering (Fig. 5D). This phenomenon directly facilitated the higher photon absorption in the hollow region that led to an improvement in electron-hole separation efficiency with remarkable photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ generation performance.
Highly efficient hydrogen evolution performance was recorded under visible LED illumination using $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon incorporated H-UiO-66 heterostructures. The plausible mechanism for water splitting over $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped H-UiO-66 was proposed and shown in Scheme 1. As seen here, the novel hollow morphology of the synthesized UiO-66 NPs facilitated the adsorption of water molecules onto the surface of the catalyst and subsequent penetration into the inner side (passed into the carbon surface) due to the $\pi$ -polar interaction between water and carbon. Under visible LED light irradiation, the electrons on the VB of H-UiO-66 were excited under visible LED light and transferred to the CB, leaving holes on VB. It is important for charge carriers to reach the catalyst surface without recombination for highly efficient photocatalytic performance.
The photoexcited electrons and holes move to both the inside and outside surfaces to react with water by suppressed recombination, due to a decrease in mass transfer resistance in the shell compared to conventional MOFs. In addition, separated electrons moved throughout carbon as a result of high conductivity, which resulted in facilitated electron transfer and inhibition of charge recombination [32]. Moreover, increase in absorbed light intensity by hollow morphology produced more electron-hole pairs with prolonged life time because of the scattering effect of the cavity inside the hollow photocatalyst. In order to split water, the CB potential of photocatalyst should be more negative than hydrogen electrode potential $(2\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=-0.41\ \mathrm{eV}.$ ) and VB should be more positive than oxygen electrode potential $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/0_{2}=0.82\ \mathrm{eV})$ . In our study, CB potential of H-UiO-66 $(-0.45\mathrm{eV})$ was more negative than hydrogen electrode potential, ensuring the necessity for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution on Pt surface. $0_{2}$ was also produced on the catalyst surface due to having more positive oxygen electrode potential $(2.05~\mathrm{eV})$ . Moreover, the high PEC performance could be attributed to the cavity effect of hollow morphology, light scattering, and presence of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon, which promotes electron transfer and suppresses electron-hole recombination.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, bio-inspired polydopamine (pDA) nanoparticles are utilized as starting core materials carrying carbon and nitrogen sources for the synthesis of hollow UiO-66 metal organicframeworks (H-UiO-66). The hollow morphology together with the light confined in a cavity (containing $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon) provided an efficient electron-hole separation for highly enhanced photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting under LED irradiation. Tafel slopes of H-UiO-66 and pristine UiO-66 photoanodes under LED illumination are calculated as 34.92 and $95.4~\mathrm{mV}_{i}$ /dec, suggesting an efficient charge transfer of H-UiO-66 photoanodes. Furthermore, the obtained electron life time (H-UiO-66:46 ms $>$ pristine $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{:}18\ \mathrm{ms}.$ ) clearly demonstrates inhibition in electron-hole recombination, leading to enrichment in charge transfer thanks to the rapid electron transfer ability of the graphitic carbon layer, and prolonged life time of light inside the hollow structure. This novel study proposed a simple fabrication technique for construction of visible-light driven shape-defined heterostructures for enhanced photoelectrochemical hydrogen generation.
